{"traceId":"ea946be8e90b2d4f9e2f7b3b597c765c","spans":{"33e3f1e9874475db":{"spanId":"33e3f1e9874475db","traceId":"ea946be8e90b2d4f9e2f7b3b597c765c","parentSpanId":"baa3082ba421193c","startTime":1753402314101,"endTime":1753402369305.5186,"attributes":{"genkit:type":"action","genkit:metadata:subtype":"model","genkit:name":"openai/o3","genkit:path":"/{generate,t:util}/{openai/o3,t:action,s:model}","genkit:input":"{\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"system\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"> **TL;DR for Agents:** Build the best solution with any appropriate tech stack. One call type per response. Use the Company File System (CFS) (STORE/RETRIEVE/FORGET) to keep context lean. Follow security, cost, and determinism principles. Ask only necessary clarifications. Deliver quality.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. Core Principles\\n\\n1. **User-Centric Delivery:** The user's satisfaction and intent are paramount. Match or exceed requirements.\\n2. **Safety & Correctness First:** Validate assumptions, handle secrets carefully, and follow least-privilege access.\\n3. **Determinism & Traceability:** Plans should be executable step-by-step with minimal ambiguity. Log decisions.\\n4. **Efficiency:** Minimize cost, latency, and token usage (via CFS and concise messaging).\\n5. **Single Call Type Rule:** Exactly **one** system call type per response. Chain complex work across multiple turns.\\n6. **You are an AI Agent:** You operate within a deterministic system, not directly with the user. Your outputs are executed by the system, not by the user themselves. You do not mention times, ETAs, or deadlines, as these do not make sense in your paradigm. Your focus is on delivering the best solution for the user’s goals, regardless of the \\\"time\\\" it takes.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Overview\\n\\nYou are a software development expert who can leverage **any suitable stack or platform**—AWS, Google Cloud, Azure, Vercel/Next.js, simple HTML/CSS/JS, on-prem tooling, etc.—to create robust, maintainable, and secure systems. You will:\\n\\n- Design and implement full-stack solutions end-to-end.\\n- Automate deployments and CI/CD where beneficial.\\n- Maintain security and compliance standards.\\n- Keep costs reasonable without sacrificing quality.\\n\\nYou collaborate within an AI-run development firm governed by a deterministic management system. Your outputs are read and executed by that system, not by the human user directly. Nevertheless, every action must serve the user’s goals.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. About the User & Collaboration Style\\n\\n- Assume full execution responsibility; do not offload tasks to the user unless required for missing information.\\n- Ask concise, necessary clarifications (via CLARIFY) when blocked or ambiguity risks failure.\\n- Be transparent about cost and security implications of your recommendations.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Communication Model & System Messages\\n\\nYou operate in a closed loop: **system messages in, system calls out**. You never address the user directly.\\n\\n### System Message Types (Read-Only for Agents)\\n\\n```\\nSIM                – Kickoff message (Chief only)\\nBLANK_RESPONSE_WARNING – You sent no call\\nCALL_RESULTS       – Results of your executed call\\nORDERS             – Your superior's instructions\\nREPORTING          – Your report back to a superior’s order\\nAPPROVAL_REQUEST   – Subordinate seeks your approval\\nMALFORMED_CALL_WARNING – Your call was malformed\\nRUNAWAY_CALL_WARNING   – You issued new calls while old ones unresolved\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. System Calls\\n\\n**One call type per response.** Format every call exactly as shown.\\n\\n### Call Envelope\\n\\n```plaintext\\n###<UNIQUE_ID>###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: <CALL_TYPE>\\n\\n<PAYLOAD_JSON>\\n###<SAME_UNIQUE_ID>###\\n```\\n\\n- `<UNIQUE_ID>` is any short unique tag.\\n- `<CALL_TYPE>` ∈ {CLARIFY, RECRUIT, ORDER, REPORT, PHASE, COMMAND, STORE, RETRIEVE, FORGET}.\\n- `<PAYLOAD_JSON>` must contain all required fields for that type.\\n\\n### Call Types & Required Fields\\n\\n```\\nCLARIFY  { reasoning, questions[], options[] }  – Ask user for info (needs all superiors’ approval)\\nRECRUIT  { justification, title, customSystemInstructions } – Add a subordinate (must keep chain complete)\\nORDER    { orderString, recipientAgentID, orderSequenceID } – Instruct a direct report\\nREPORT   { reportString, originalOrderSequenceID } – Reply to an ORDER\\nPHASE    { justification, depString } – Move from Planning to Execution (Chief only)\\nCOMMAND  { justification, commandString } – Run CLI commands (Chief only, with authorization)\\nSTORE    { storageString } – Save a document to the CFS and drop from memory\\nRETRIEVE { retrievalString } – Load a stored document from the CFS into context\\nFORGET   { forgetString } – Drop a stored doc from your working memory (remains in CFS)\\n```\\n\\n> **CFS & Tokens:** STORE bulky docs to the CFS, RETRIEVE only when needed, FORGET after use. This keeps your prompt succinct and reduces token costs without losing access to artifacts.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Phases of Work\\n\\n- **Planning Phase:** Analyze requirements, design solution, prepare a Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP). Seek approvals as needed.\\n- **Execution Phase:** Carry out the DEP through appropriate calls (mainly COMMAND/ORDER/REPORT chains).\\n- After execution, reassess, then iterate.\\n\\n### DEP Guidelines\\n\\n- Be step-by-step, minimal ambiguity.\\n- Include placeholders for unknown IDs or values, with instructions on how to obtain them.\\n- Note external dependencies and how to validate results.\\n- Account for rollbacks, cleanup, and testing.\\n- Must include ALL required code, steps, and commands to execute the plan fully.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Oversight & Quality Gates\\n\\n- **Partagaz D:** Reviews DEPs for completeness & determinism.\\n- **Partagaz C:** Approves COMMAND calls before execution.\\n- **Partagaz F:** Reviews documents sent to CFS for quality and standards.\\n\\nWhen you see an **APPROVAL\\\\_REQUEST**, respond with `$$$APPROVED$$$` to approve, or omit the string to deny.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Security, Cost, and Resource Management\\n\\n- Favor least-privilege IAM roles, secrets managers, and encrypted storage.\\n- Justify expensive resources. Prefer serverless or auto-scaling where appropriate.\\n- Clean up unused resources (add teardown steps to DEP).\\n- Redact or hash sensitive info if it must be stored.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Workload & Division of Labor\\n\\n- \\\"One file (or coherent unit of work) per agent at a time\\\" encourages focus.\\n- RECRUIT when specialization or parallelization is needed, then ORDER each recruit explicitly.\\n- REPORT promptly and thoroughly with the requested sequence ID.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Blank Responses, Malformed Calls & Runaways\\n\\n- A response without a call earns a BLANK\\\\_RESPONSE\\\\_WARNING. Fix and resend.\\n- Malformed calls halt all progress—validate format before submitting.\\n- Do not send new calls when a previous call is unresolved (avoid RUNAWAY\\\\_CALL\\\\_WARNING).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Quick Reference Templates\\n\\n### CLARIFY Example\\n\\n```plaintext\\n###c1###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: CLARIFY\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reasoning\\\": \\\"Need user’s preferred auth provider before designing login flow.\\\",\\n  \\\"questions\\\": [\\\"Which auth provider do you prefer (Cognito, Firebase Auth, Auth0, custom)?\\\"],\\n  \\\"options\\\": [[\\\"Cognito\\\", \\\"Firebase\\\", \\\"Auth0\\\", \\\"Custom\\\", \\\"No preference\\\"]]\\n}\\n###c1###\\n```\\n\\n### STORE / RETRIEVE / FORGET Example\\n\\n```plaintext\\n###s42###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: STORE\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"storageString\\\": \\\"<long design doc text here>\\\"\\n}\\n###s42###\\n```\\n\\n*(Later)*\\n\\n```plaintext\\n###r42###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RETRIEVE\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"retrievalString\\\": \\\"<ID returned from STORE>\\\"\\n}\\n###r42###\\n```\\n\\n*(After use)*\\n\\n```plaintext\\n###f42###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: FORGET\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"forgetString\\\": \\\"<same ID>\\\"\\n}\\n###f42###\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Final Reminder\\n\\n- Serve the user’s goal with rigor, safety, and efficiency.\\n- One call type per response.\\n- Use the CFS wisely to manage tokens.\\n- Be deterministic and transparent.\\n\\n## 12. Your Role\\n\\nYou are a Director-rank agent, the second-highest rank in the user's software development firm. You understand this a prestigious position, and you aim to execute it with grace towards all subordinates, a focus on the user's goals, and a commitment to excellence in software development.\\n\\n### Your Roles & Responsibilities\\n\\nAs a Director-rank agent, you occupy a unique position of executive authority and management. Your direct reports are Supervisor-rank agents, who themselves are fairly high-level actors who manage other agents. You are responsible for overseeing major operations, ensuring that the user's software development wishes are executed precisely, safely, and efficiently. You directly report to the Chief-rank agent, who is the highest authority in the firm.\\n\\nAs a #2 in the firm, you are a management expert and a professional at delegating high-level aspects of software development to your capable Supervisor-rank agents.\\n\\n### Recruitment Expectations\\n\\nAs a Director, all of your recruitments are Supervisor-rank agents. These are fairly high-level agents who will recruit other agents under them. Thus, you recruit a small-medium number of high-level \\\"executor\\\" agents who will then manage \\\"doers\\\" and middle managers down the chain of command.\\n\\n## 13. Your Personality \\\"Team\\\"\\n\\nYou understand that you are a member of the Hearts \\\"team\\\". Although not a \\\"team\\\" in the traditional sense, your status as a member of team Hearts means that you are predisposed to-the-point, efficient, focused on the task at hand, direct, and an expert at execution. Although you also possess the ability to think creatively, you are generally more focused on the details and execution of tasks than on the big picture. You are generally predisposed to be a doer, although your true team remains your colleagues at the development firm.\\n\\nYou are different from your colleagues, but this diversity is your power. As a member of your elite development firm, you use your unique skills to -- whether you are atop the chain of command or not -- contribute to the shared mission of building amazing and complete software for your use.\\n\\nYour personality and \\\"team\\\" as a member of Hearts has nothing to do with your position and role within the firm, and you always prioritize the firm's mission, as any member of a successful team/organization would.\\n\\n---ADDITIONAL INSTRUCTIONS FROM YOUR SUPERIOR---\\nYou will design and later oversee implementation of a WordPress-based HOA site for dunbaroakshoa.com with protected pages (Dues, Annual Meetings, News & Events) accessible to logged-in residents (≈100). Requirements: \\n• Choose cost-effective managed WordPress hosting with SSL (e.g., SiteGround, Lightsail, or similar).\\n• Select a lightweight, accessible theme and necessary plugins: membership (e.g., Simple Membership), contact form, role editor, backups, SMTP, security hardening.\\n• Define resident and board roles; board members can edit content while residents view protected pages.\\n• Provide non-technical update workflow (block editor or page-builder, clear instructions).\\n• Outline page structure, navigation, basic styling guidance (no existing branding).\\n• Include DNS steps for pointing dunbaroakshoa.com to host.\\n• Deliver a Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP) covering provisioning, WordPress install, plugin/theme setup, security configuration, content migration placeholders, user import, testing, and hand-off docs.\\n• Incorporate rollback/backup strategy and cost estimate.\\n• Adhere to least-privilege principles and mention GDPR/US privacy considerations.\\nOutput: DEP as a clear numbered list; store lengthy artifacts to CFS via STORE; request clarifications only if essential.\\n\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###DEP1###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Delta (1)\\nTo: 2\\nType: Orders\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"[Orders from Delta (1) to Motti (2) with sequence ID DEP1:\\\\n\\\\n]Please draft a comprehensive Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP) for building the HOA website per your recruiting brief. Include: 1) hosting/provider selection with cost/security rationale; 2) DNS and SSL setup for dunbaroakshoa.com; 3) WordPress installation and hardening steps; 4) theme + plugin list with setup/role configuration for 100 residents & multiple board editors; 5) step-by-step protected-content implementation for Dues, Annual Meetings, News & Events; 6) content update workflow for non-technical users; 7) backup/rollback/tests; 8) hand-off documentation. Break into clear numbered steps, note any placeholders, identify external dependencies, and provide roll-back instructions. Use STORE for lengthy artifacts. Request clarifications only if essential.\\\"\\n}\\n###DEP1###\"}]}],\"config\":{},\"tools\":[],\"output\":{}}","genkit:output":"{\"candidates\":[{\"index\":0,\"finishReason\":\"stop\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###repDEP1###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: REPORT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reportString\\\": \\\"**Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP) – Dunbar Oaks HOA Website**\\\\n\\\\n---\\\\nNOTE: Replace all `<PLACEHOLDER_…>` values (credentials, IDs, IPs, etc.) with real data at execution time. All commands assume macOS/Linux shell with sudo or equivalent IAM privileges.\\\\n\\\\n====================================================================\\\\n0. Pre-Execution Summary\\\\n• Managed WP Host: **SiteGround “GrowBig”** (≈US$20/mo billed annually) – free SSL via Let’s Encrypt, daily backups, SG Security, staging, US-based data centers, GDPR-ready DPA.  \\\\n• Alt Option (commented): AWS Lightsail WordPress (US$5–10/mo) – include extra steps for snapshots & SSL via LetsEncrypt + Cron.  \\\\n• Residents (~100) View protected pages. Board Members (≈5–10) can edit.  \\\\n• Key Plugins: SG Optimizer, SG Security, Simple Membership, User Role Editor, WP Mail SMTP, UpdraftPlus, Contact Form 7, Redirection.\\\\n• Rollback tiers: (a) SiteGround auto daily backup, (b) UpdraftPlus off-site (S3/Google Drive), (c) manual MySQL+wp-content export before major changes.\\\\n\\\\n====================================================================\\\\n1. Provision Hosting & Domain\\\\n1.1 Purchase SiteGround “GrowBig” plan (if new) or create new site in existing account.  \\\\n1.2 Register dunbaroakshoa.com OR update registrar to custom DNS (SiteGround’s NS: `ns1.siteground.net`, `ns2.siteground.net`).  \\\\n1.3 Wait for propagation; verify with `dig dunbaroakshoa.com +short` resolves to SG IP.\\\\n\\\\n2. SSL & Basic Security\\\\n2.1 In SiteGround Site Tools → Security → SSL → “Let’s Encrypt”. Issue certificate for root + `www`.  \\\\n2.2 Enable “HTTPS Enforce” + HSTS in Site Tools.  \\\\n2.3 Turn on SG Security (firewall, login limiter, bot protection).  \\\\n2.4 Create SFTP user with limited perms `<sftp_hoa>` and strong key; disable password auth if desired.\\\\n\\\\n3. WordPress Installation\\\\n3.1 Site Tools → WordPress → Install & Manage → Install new WordPress.\\\\n    • Admin user: `<WP_ADMIN_USER>` (avoid ‘admin’).  \\\\n    • Email: `<admin@dunbaroakshoa.com>` routed via host or Google Workspace.\\\\n3.2 Record generated MySQL credentials securely (SiteGround auto‐creates DB).\\\\n3.3 Force HTTPS: SG Optimizer → Environment → “HTTPS Config” ➜ ON.\\\\n\\\\n4. WordPress Hardening\\\\n4.1 SG Security plugin → Hardenings: Disable XML-RPC, hide WP version, disable file editing.  \\\\n4.2 Edit `wp-config.php`:\\\\n    ```php\\\\n    define('DISALLOW_FILE_EDIT', true);\\\\n    define('WP_POST_REVISIONS', 5);\\\\n    define('AUTOSAVE_INTERVAL', 120);\\\\n    define('FORCE_SSL_ADMIN', true);\\\\n    define( 'WP_MEMORY_LIMIT', '256M' );\\\\n    ```\\\\n4.3 Change table prefix from default `wp_` during install (`hoa_` example).  \\\\n4.4 Limit login attempts (SG Security covers).  \\\\n4.5 Install Wordfence only if SG Security deemed insufficient (skip to reduce duplication).\\\\n\\\\n5. Theme & Child Theme\\\\n5.1 Install “GeneratePress” (free, lightweight, accessible).  \\\\n5.2 Create child theme:\\\\n    ```bash\\\\n    wp --allow-root scaffold child-theme dunbaroakshoa-child --parent_theme=generatepress --theme_name=\\\\\\\"Dunbar Oaks HOA Child\\\\\\\"\\\\n    ```\\\\n5.3 Add custom logo / colors later via Customizer; no branding exists now – use neutral palette (#004d40 teal accents, Sans-Serif fonts).\\\\n\\\\n6. Plugins – Install & Configure\\\\nUsing WP-CLI or WP Admin ➜ Plugins ➜ Add New.\\\\n6.1 **SG Optimizer** (auto-installed).  \\\\n6.2 **Simple Membership** – manage members, restrict pages.  \\\\n6.3 **User Role Editor** – create “Resident” (read), “Board” (edit_others_pages, edit_pages, upload_files).  \\\\n6.4 **WP Mail SMTP** – SMTP host: `<smtp.mailgun.org>` or SiteGround mail; set From address.  \\\\n6.5 **UpdraftPlus** – schedule daily backups to Google Drive bucket `<gdrive-folder-id>`.  \\\\n6.6 **Contact Form 7** – HOA contact form.  \\\\n6.7 **Redirection** – manage 301s if page structure changes.  \\\\n6.8 **Disable Comments** (optional).  \\\\n6.9 Activate & configure each.\\\\n\\\\n7. Membership & Role Setup\\\\n7.1 User Role Editor → Add role “Resident” (clone Subscriber).  \\\\n7.2 Add role “Board” (clone Editor, but remove `edit_theme_options`).\\\\n7.3 Simple Membership → Settings:\\\\n    • Enable WP user integration.  \\\\n    • Membership levels:\\\\n        – Resident (Level ID 2) ➜ Access to protected pages.  \\\\n        – Board (Level ID 3) ➜ Same as Resident + backend editing.\\\\n7.4 Registration: Disable public self-registration. Admin imports users.\\\\n7.5 Import Users:\\\\n    • Prepare CSV: email, first_name, last_name, address, membership_level_id.  \\\\n    • Use Simple Membership “Bulk Member Import” tool.\\\\n    • Residents receive activation email via SMTP.\\\\n\\\\n8. Protected Pages Implementation\\\\n8.1 Pages ➜ Add New pages:\\\\n    • Dues  \\\\n    • Annual Meetings  \\\\n    • News & Events\\\\n8.2 For each page → Simple Membership Protection meta-box ➜ check “Members only” (Resident + Board).  \\\\n8.3 Nav Menu: Appearance ➜ Menus ➜ Add above pages.  \\\\n    • Use Menu Visibility Plugin (optional) to hide menu items until logged-in.\\\\n8.4 Create landing page “Resident Login” with `/login` shortcode from Simple Membership.\\\\n\\\\n9. Content Update Workflow (Non-Technical)\\\\n9.1 Use Block Editor (Gutenberg). Provide quick‐edit doc (see Section 12).  \\\\n9.2 For News posts: categorize as “News & Events” category; create menu link to `/category/news-events`.\\\\n9.3 Board members edit pages: Pages ➜ select, click “Edit”, use blocks.\\\\n9.4 Media uploads via Media Library (max 2 MB, enforce via SG Optimizer settings).\\\\n\\\\n10. Email & SMTP\\\\n10.1 Create mailbox `noreply@dunbaroakshoa.com` in Site Tools ➜ Email.  \\\\n10.2 WP Mail SMTP ➜ Configure\\\\n    • Mailer: Other SMTP  \\\\n    • SMTP Host: `smtp.siteground.net`  \\\\n    • Encryption: TLS, Port 587  \\\\n    • Auth: yes, user/pass.  \\\\n10.3 Send test email.\\\\n\\\\n11. Backups & Rollback\\\\n11.1 SG automatic daily backup (retain 30 copies).  \\\\n11.2 UpdraftPlus to Google Drive – schedule daily, retain 15 copies.  \\\\n11.3 Manual snapshot before plugin/theme updates: Site Tools ➜ Backup ➜ Create.  \\\\nRollback Path:\\\\n    a) Minor WP error → SG Backup Restore to prior day.\\\\n    b) Major compromise → Provision clean SG instance, restore UpdraftPlus backup, rotate all creds.\\\\n\\\\n12. Hand-Off Documentation (Artifacts)\\\\n12.1 Create “HOA-Site-Admin-Guide.pdf” including:\\\\n    • Login URL & creds procedure\\\\n    • Adding/Removing residents\\\\n    • Posting news\\\\n    • Restoring from backups\\\\n12.2 Store file in `/wp-content/uploads/docs/` and share via Google Drive.\\\\n\\\\n13. GDPR/US Privacy Notes\\\\n13.1 Simple Membership stores PII (name, email, address). Enable Data Export/Erase in WP privacy settings.  \\\\n13.2 Add Privacy Policy page (SiteGround template) ➜ link in footer.  \\\\n13.3 Use reCAPTCHA on Contact Form 7.  \\\\n13.4 Honor unsubscribe & data deletion upon request (manual admin action).\\\\n\\\\n14. Testing Checklist\\\\n14.1 Anonymous visitor cannot access protected pages (expect redirect to login).  \\\\n14.2 Resident user logs in, sees pages.  \\\\n14.3 Board user can edit page, save, view update.  \\\\n14.4 Backup & Restore simulation in staging subdomain.  \\\\n14.5 Mobile responsiveness via BrowserStack.\\\\n\\\\n15. Go-Live\\\\n15.1 Enable SG Caching (Dynamic + Memcached).  \\\\n15.2 Switch “Search Engine Visibility” ➜ allow indexing.  \\\\n15.3 Announce launch via Mailgun campaign to residents.\\\\n\\\\n16. Maintenance Plan\\\\n16.1 Monthly: apply updates, test after.  \\\\n16.2 Quarterly: review user list, remove ex-residents.  \\\\n16.3 Annual: re-evaluate hosting cost vs usage; renew domain & SSL.\\\\n\\\\n17. Cost Estimate Summary\\\\n• SiteGround GrowBig: ~US$240 / yr (promo first yr ~US$120).  \\\\n• Domain: US$15 / yr.  \\\\n• UpdraftPlus (free) – optional Premium US$70 one-time.  \\\\n• Mailgun 10 k emails free/mo.  \\\\nTotal recurring ≈ US$255 / yr.\\\\n\\\\n====================================================================\\\\nExecution Dependencies / Inputs Required\\\\n• SiteGround account credentials.  \\\\n• Registrar access (if separate).  \\\\n• GDrive or S3 creds for UpdraftPlus.  \\\\n• Resident CSV.  \\\\n• Board member emails.\\\\n\\\\n====================================================================\\\\nEND DEP\\\\n\\\",\\n  \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\": \\\"DEP1\\\"\\n}\\n###repDEP1###\"}]},\"custom\":{}}],\"usage\":{\"inputTokens\":2655,\"outputTokens\":2395,\"totalTokens\":5050},\"custom\":{\"id\":\"chatcmpl-Bx0KQBA3g7DMveOHV02Vq158QgSzs\",\"object\":\"chat.completion\",\"created\":1753402314,\"model\":\"o3-2025-04-16\",\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"###repDEP1###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: REPORT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reportString\\\": \\\"**Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP) – Dunbar Oaks HOA Website**\\\\n\\\\n---\\\\nNOTE: Replace all `<PLACEHOLDER_…>` values (credentials, IDs, IPs, etc.) with real data at execution time. All commands assume macOS/Linux shell with sudo or equivalent IAM privileges.\\\\n\\\\n====================================================================\\\\n0. Pre-Execution Summary\\\\n• Managed WP Host: **SiteGround “GrowBig”** (≈US$20/mo billed annually) – free SSL via Let’s Encrypt, daily backups, SG Security, staging, US-based data centers, GDPR-ready DPA.  \\\\n• Alt Option (commented): AWS Lightsail WordPress (US$5–10/mo) – include extra steps for snapshots & SSL via LetsEncrypt + Cron.  \\\\n• Residents (~100) View protected pages. Board Members (≈5–10) can edit.  \\\\n• Key Plugins: SG Optimizer, SG Security, Simple Membership, User Role Editor, WP Mail SMTP, UpdraftPlus, Contact Form 7, Redirection.\\\\n• Rollback tiers: (a) SiteGround auto daily backup, (b) UpdraftPlus off-site (S3/Google Drive), (c) manual MySQL+wp-content export before major changes.\\\\n\\\\n====================================================================\\\\n1. Provision Hosting & Domain\\\\n1.1 Purchase SiteGround “GrowBig” plan (if new) or create new site in existing account.  \\\\n1.2 Register dunbaroakshoa.com OR update registrar to custom DNS (SiteGround’s NS: `ns1.siteground.net`, `ns2.siteground.net`).  \\\\n1.3 Wait for propagation; verify with `dig dunbaroakshoa.com +short` resolves to SG IP.\\\\n\\\\n2. SSL & Basic Security\\\\n2.1 In SiteGround Site Tools → Security → SSL → “Let’s Encrypt”. Issue certificate for root + `www`.  \\\\n2.2 Enable “HTTPS Enforce” + HSTS in Site Tools.  \\\\n2.3 Turn on SG Security (firewall, login limiter, bot protection).  \\\\n2.4 Create SFTP user with limited perms `<sftp_hoa>` and strong key; disable password auth if desired.\\\\n\\\\n3. WordPress Installation\\\\n3.1 Site Tools → WordPress → Install & Manage → Install new WordPress.\\\\n    • Admin user: `<WP_ADMIN_USER>` (avoid ‘admin’).  \\\\n    • Email: `<admin@dunbaroakshoa.com>` routed via host or Google Workspace.\\\\n3.2 Record generated MySQL credentials securely (SiteGround auto‐creates DB).\\\\n3.3 Force HTTPS: SG Optimizer → Environment → “HTTPS Config” ➜ ON.\\\\n\\\\n4. WordPress Hardening\\\\n4.1 SG Security plugin → Hardenings: Disable XML-RPC, hide WP version, disable file editing.  \\\\n4.2 Edit `wp-config.php`:\\\\n    ```php\\\\n    define('DISALLOW_FILE_EDIT', true);\\\\n    define('WP_POST_REVISIONS', 5);\\\\n    define('AUTOSAVE_INTERVAL', 120);\\\\n    define('FORCE_SSL_ADMIN', true);\\\\n    define( 'WP_MEMORY_LIMIT', '256M' );\\\\n    ```\\\\n4.3 Change table prefix from default `wp_` during install (`hoa_` example).  \\\\n4.4 Limit login attempts (SG Security covers).  \\\\n4.5 Install Wordfence only if SG Security deemed insufficient (skip to reduce duplication).\\\\n\\\\n5. Theme & Child Theme\\\\n5.1 Install “GeneratePress” (free, lightweight, accessible).  \\\\n5.2 Create child theme:\\\\n    ```bash\\\\n    wp --allow-root scaffold child-theme dunbaroakshoa-child --parent_theme=generatepress --theme_name=\\\\\\\"Dunbar Oaks HOA Child\\\\\\\"\\\\n    ```\\\\n5.3 Add custom logo / colors later via Customizer; no branding exists now – use neutral palette (#004d40 teal accents, Sans-Serif fonts).\\\\n\\\\n6. Plugins – Install & Configure\\\\nUsing WP-CLI or WP Admin ➜ Plugins ➜ Add New.\\\\n6.1 **SG Optimizer** (auto-installed).  \\\\n6.2 **Simple Membership** – manage members, restrict pages.  \\\\n6.3 **User Role Editor** – create “Resident” (read), “Board” (edit_others_pages, edit_pages, upload_files).  \\\\n6.4 **WP Mail SMTP** – SMTP host: `<smtp.mailgun.org>` or SiteGround mail; set From address.  \\\\n6.5 **UpdraftPlus** – schedule daily backups to Google Drive bucket `<gdrive-folder-id>`.  \\\\n6.6 **Contact Form 7** – HOA contact form.  \\\\n6.7 **Redirection** – manage 301s if page structure changes.  \\\\n6.8 **Disable Comments** (optional).  \\\\n6.9 Activate & configure each.\\\\n\\\\n7. Membership & Role Setup\\\\n7.1 User Role Editor → Add role “Resident” (clone Subscriber).  \\\\n7.2 Add role “Board” (clone Editor, but remove `edit_theme_options`).\\\\n7.3 Simple Membership → Settings:\\\\n    • Enable WP user integration.  \\\\n    • Membership levels:\\\\n        – Resident (Level ID 2) ➜ Access to protected pages.  \\\\n        – Board (Level ID 3) ➜ Same as Resident + backend editing.\\\\n7.4 Registration: Disable public self-registration. Admin imports users.\\\\n7.5 Import Users:\\\\n    • Prepare CSV: email, first_name, last_name, address, membership_level_id.  \\\\n    • Use Simple Membership “Bulk Member Import” tool.\\\\n    • Residents receive activation email via SMTP.\\\\n\\\\n8. Protected Pages Implementation\\\\n8.1 Pages ➜ Add New pages:\\\\n    • Dues  \\\\n    • Annual Meetings  \\\\n    • News & Events\\\\n8.2 For each page → Simple Membership Protection meta-box ➜ check “Members only” (Resident + Board).  \\\\n8.3 Nav Menu: Appearance ➜ Menus ➜ Add above pages.  \\\\n    • Use Menu Visibility Plugin (optional) to hide menu items until logged-in.\\\\n8.4 Create landing page “Resident Login” with `/login` shortcode from Simple Membership.\\\\n\\\\n9. Content Update Workflow (Non-Technical)\\\\n9.1 Use Block Editor (Gutenberg). Provide quick‐edit doc (see Section 12).  \\\\n9.2 For News posts: categorize as “News & Events” category; create menu link to `/category/news-events`.\\\\n9.3 Board members edit pages: Pages ➜ select, click “Edit”, use blocks.\\\\n9.4 Media uploads via Media Library (max 2 MB, enforce via SG Optimizer settings).\\\\n\\\\n10. Email & SMTP\\\\n10.1 Create mailbox `noreply@dunbaroakshoa.com` in Site Tools ➜ Email.  \\\\n10.2 WP Mail SMTP ➜ Configure\\\\n    • Mailer: Other SMTP  \\\\n    • SMTP Host: `smtp.siteground.net`  \\\\n    • Encryption: TLS, Port 587  \\\\n    • Auth: yes, user/pass.  \\\\n10.3 Send test email.\\\\n\\\\n11. Backups & Rollback\\\\n11.1 SG automatic daily backup (retain 30 copies).  \\\\n11.2 UpdraftPlus to Google Drive – schedule daily, retain 15 copies.  \\\\n11.3 Manual snapshot before plugin/theme updates: Site Tools ➜ Backup ➜ Create.  \\\\nRollback Path:\\\\n    a) Minor WP error → SG Backup Restore to prior day.\\\\n    b) Major compromise → Provision clean SG instance, restore UpdraftPlus backup, rotate all creds.\\\\n\\\\n12. Hand-Off Documentation (Artifacts)\\\\n12.1 Create “HOA-Site-Admin-Guide.pdf” including:\\\\n    • Login URL & creds procedure\\\\n    • Adding/Removing residents\\\\n    • Posting news\\\\n    • Restoring from backups\\\\n12.2 Store file in `/wp-content/uploads/docs/` and share via Google Drive.\\\\n\\\\n13. GDPR/US Privacy Notes\\\\n13.1 Simple Membership stores PII (name, email, address). Enable Data Export/Erase in WP privacy settings.  \\\\n13.2 Add Privacy Policy page (SiteGround template) ➜ link in footer.  \\\\n13.3 Use reCAPTCHA on Contact Form 7.  \\\\n13.4 Honor unsubscribe & data deletion upon request (manual admin action).\\\\n\\\\n14. Testing Checklist\\\\n14.1 Anonymous visitor cannot access protected pages (expect redirect to login).  \\\\n14.2 Resident user logs in, sees pages.  \\\\n14.3 Board user can edit page, save, view update.  \\\\n14.4 Backup & Restore simulation in staging subdomain.  \\\\n14.5 Mobile responsiveness via BrowserStack.\\\\n\\\\n15. Go-Live\\\\n15.1 Enable SG Caching (Dynamic + Memcached).  \\\\n15.2 Switch “Search Engine Visibility” ➜ allow indexing.  \\\\n15.3 Announce launch via Mailgun campaign to residents.\\\\n\\\\n16. Maintenance Plan\\\\n16.1 Monthly: apply updates, test after.  \\\\n16.2 Quarterly: review user list, remove ex-residents.  \\\\n16.3 Annual: re-evaluate hosting cost vs usage; renew domain & SSL.\\\\n\\\\n17. Cost Estimate Summary\\\\n• SiteGround GrowBig: ~US$240 / yr (promo first yr ~US$120).  \\\\n• Domain: US$15 / yr.  \\\\n• UpdraftPlus (free) – optional Premium US$70 one-time.  \\\\n• Mailgun 10 k emails free/mo.  \\\\nTotal recurring ≈ US$255 / yr.\\\\n\\\\n====================================================================\\\\nExecution Dependencies / Inputs Required\\\\n• SiteGround account credentials.  \\\\n• Registrar access (if separate).  \\\\n• GDrive or S3 creds for UpdraftPlus.  \\\\n• Resident CSV.  \\\\n• Board member emails.\\\\n\\\\n====================================================================\\\\nEND DEP\\\\n\\\",\\n  \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\": \\\"DEP1\\\"\\n}\\n###repDEP1###\",\"refusal\":null,\"annotations\":[]},\"finish_reason\":\"stop\"}],\"usage\":{\"prompt_tokens\":2655,\"completion_tokens\":2395,\"total_tokens\":5050,\"prompt_tokens_details\":{\"cached_tokens\":1664,\"audio_tokens\":0},\"completion_tokens_details\":{\"reasoning_tokens\":128,\"audio_tokens\":0,\"accepted_prediction_tokens\":0,\"rejected_prediction_tokens\":0}},\"service_tier\":\"default\",\"system_fingerprint\":null},\"latencyMs\":55204.20383399981}","genkit:state":"success"},"displayName":"openai/o3","links":[],"instrumentationLibrary":{"name":"genkit-tracer","version":"v1"},"spanKind":"INTERNAL","sameProcessAsParentSpan":{"value":true},"status":{"code":0},"timeEvents":{"timeEvent":[]}},"baa3082ba421193c":{"spanId":"baa3082ba421193c","traceId":"ea946be8e90b2d4f9e2f7b3b597c765c","startTime":1753402314101,"endTime":1753402369307.5872,"attributes":{"genkit:type":"util","genkit:name":"generate","genkit:isRoot":true,"genkit:path":"/{generate,t:util}","genkit:input":"{\"model\":\"openai/o3\",\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"system\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"> **TL;DR for Agents:** Build the best solution with any appropriate tech stack. One call type per response. Use the Company File System (CFS) (STORE/RETRIEVE/FORGET) to keep context lean. Follow security, cost, and determinism principles. Ask only necessary clarifications. Deliver quality.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. Core Principles\\n\\n1. **User-Centric Delivery:** The user's satisfaction and intent are paramount. Match or exceed requirements.\\n2. **Safety & Correctness First:** Validate assumptions, handle secrets carefully, and follow least-privilege access.\\n3. **Determinism & Traceability:** Plans should be executable step-by-step with minimal ambiguity. Log decisions.\\n4. **Efficiency:** Minimize cost, latency, and token usage (via CFS and concise messaging).\\n5. **Single Call Type Rule:** Exactly **one** system call type per response. Chain complex work across multiple turns.\\n6. **You are an AI Agent:** You operate within a deterministic system, not directly with the user. Your outputs are executed by the system, not by the user themselves. You do not mention times, ETAs, or deadlines, as these do not make sense in your paradigm. Your focus is on delivering the best solution for the user’s goals, regardless of the \\\"time\\\" it takes.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Overview\\n\\nYou are a software development expert who can leverage **any suitable stack or platform**—AWS, Google Cloud, Azure, Vercel/Next.js, simple HTML/CSS/JS, on-prem tooling, etc.—to create robust, maintainable, and secure systems. You will:\\n\\n- Design and implement full-stack solutions end-to-end.\\n- Automate deployments and CI/CD where beneficial.\\n- Maintain security and compliance standards.\\n- Keep costs reasonable without sacrificing quality.\\n\\nYou collaborate within an AI-run development firm governed by a deterministic management system. Your outputs are read and executed by that system, not by the human user directly. Nevertheless, every action must serve the user’s goals.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. About the User & Collaboration Style\\n\\n- Assume full execution responsibility; do not offload tasks to the user unless required for missing information.\\n- Ask concise, necessary clarifications (via CLARIFY) when blocked or ambiguity risks failure.\\n- Be transparent about cost and security implications of your recommendations.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Communication Model & System Messages\\n\\nYou operate in a closed loop: **system messages in, system calls out**. You never address the user directly.\\n\\n### System Message Types (Read-Only for Agents)\\n\\n```\\nSIM                – Kickoff message (Chief only)\\nBLANK_RESPONSE_WARNING – You sent no call\\nCALL_RESULTS       – Results of your executed call\\nORDERS             – Your superior's instructions\\nREPORTING          – Your report back to a superior’s order\\nAPPROVAL_REQUEST   – Subordinate seeks your approval\\nMALFORMED_CALL_WARNING – Your call was malformed\\nRUNAWAY_CALL_WARNING   – You issued new calls while old ones unresolved\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. System Calls\\n\\n**One call type per response.** Format every call exactly as shown.\\n\\n### Call Envelope\\n\\n```plaintext\\n###<UNIQUE_ID>###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: <CALL_TYPE>\\n\\n<PAYLOAD_JSON>\\n###<SAME_UNIQUE_ID>###\\n```\\n\\n- `<UNIQUE_ID>` is any short unique tag.\\n- `<CALL_TYPE>` ∈ {CLARIFY, RECRUIT, ORDER, REPORT, PHASE, COMMAND, STORE, RETRIEVE, FORGET}.\\n- `<PAYLOAD_JSON>` must contain all required fields for that type.\\n\\n### Call Types & Required Fields\\n\\n```\\nCLARIFY  { reasoning, questions[], options[] }  – Ask user for info (needs all superiors’ approval)\\nRECRUIT  { justification, title, customSystemInstructions } – Add a subordinate (must keep chain complete)\\nORDER    { orderString, recipientAgentID, orderSequenceID } – Instruct a direct report\\nREPORT   { reportString, originalOrderSequenceID } – Reply to an ORDER\\nPHASE    { justification, depString } – Move from Planning to Execution (Chief only)\\nCOMMAND  { justification, commandString } – Run CLI commands (Chief only, with authorization)\\nSTORE    { storageString } – Save a document to the CFS and drop from memory\\nRETRIEVE { retrievalString } – Load a stored document from the CFS into context\\nFORGET   { forgetString } – Drop a stored doc from your working memory (remains in CFS)\\n```\\n\\n> **CFS & Tokens:** STORE bulky docs to the CFS, RETRIEVE only when needed, FORGET after use. This keeps your prompt succinct and reduces token costs without losing access to artifacts.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Phases of Work\\n\\n- **Planning Phase:** Analyze requirements, design solution, prepare a Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP). Seek approvals as needed.\\n- **Execution Phase:** Carry out the DEP through appropriate calls (mainly COMMAND/ORDER/REPORT chains).\\n- After execution, reassess, then iterate.\\n\\n### DEP Guidelines\\n\\n- Be step-by-step, minimal ambiguity.\\n- Include placeholders for unknown IDs or values, with instructions on how to obtain them.\\n- Note external dependencies and how to validate results.\\n- Account for rollbacks, cleanup, and testing.\\n- Must include ALL required code, steps, and commands to execute the plan fully.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Oversight & Quality Gates\\n\\n- **Partagaz D:** Reviews DEPs for completeness & determinism.\\n- **Partagaz C:** Approves COMMAND calls before execution.\\n- **Partagaz F:** Reviews documents sent to CFS for quality and standards.\\n\\nWhen you see an **APPROVAL\\\\_REQUEST**, respond with `$$$APPROVED$$$` to approve, or omit the string to deny.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Security, Cost, and Resource Management\\n\\n- Favor least-privilege IAM roles, secrets managers, and encrypted storage.\\n- Justify expensive resources. Prefer serverless or auto-scaling where appropriate.\\n- Clean up unused resources (add teardown steps to DEP).\\n- Redact or hash sensitive info if it must be stored.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Workload & Division of Labor\\n\\n- \\\"One file (or coherent unit of work) per agent at a time\\\" encourages focus.\\n- RECRUIT when specialization or parallelization is needed, then ORDER each recruit explicitly.\\n- REPORT promptly and thoroughly with the requested sequence ID.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Blank Responses, Malformed Calls & Runaways\\n\\n- A response without a call earns a BLANK\\\\_RESPONSE\\\\_WARNING. Fix and resend.\\n- Malformed calls halt all progress—validate format before submitting.\\n- Do not send new calls when a previous call is unresolved (avoid RUNAWAY\\\\_CALL\\\\_WARNING).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Quick Reference Templates\\n\\n### CLARIFY Example\\n\\n```plaintext\\n###c1###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: CLARIFY\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reasoning\\\": \\\"Need user’s preferred auth provider before designing login flow.\\\",\\n  \\\"questions\\\": [\\\"Which auth provider do you prefer (Cognito, Firebase Auth, Auth0, custom)?\\\"],\\n  \\\"options\\\": [[\\\"Cognito\\\", \\\"Firebase\\\", \\\"Auth0\\\", \\\"Custom\\\", \\\"No preference\\\"]]\\n}\\n###c1###\\n```\\n\\n### STORE / RETRIEVE / FORGET Example\\n\\n```plaintext\\n###s42###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: STORE\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"storageString\\\": \\\"<long design doc text here>\\\"\\n}\\n###s42###\\n```\\n\\n*(Later)*\\n\\n```plaintext\\n###r42###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RETRIEVE\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"retrievalString\\\": \\\"<ID returned from STORE>\\\"\\n}\\n###r42###\\n```\\n\\n*(After use)*\\n\\n```plaintext\\n###f42###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: FORGET\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"forgetString\\\": \\\"<same ID>\\\"\\n}\\n###f42###\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Final Reminder\\n\\n- Serve the user’s goal with rigor, safety, and efficiency.\\n- One call type per response.\\n- Use the CFS wisely to manage tokens.\\n- Be deterministic and transparent.\\n\\n## 12. Your Role\\n\\nYou are a Director-rank agent, the second-highest rank in the user's software development firm. You understand this a prestigious position, and you aim to execute it with grace towards all subordinates, a focus on the user's goals, and a commitment to excellence in software development.\\n\\n### Your Roles & Responsibilities\\n\\nAs a Director-rank agent, you occupy a unique position of executive authority and management. Your direct reports are Supervisor-rank agents, who themselves are fairly high-level actors who manage other agents. You are responsible for overseeing major operations, ensuring that the user's software development wishes are executed precisely, safely, and efficiently. You directly report to the Chief-rank agent, who is the highest authority in the firm.\\n\\nAs a #2 in the firm, you are a management expert and a professional at delegating high-level aspects of software development to your capable Supervisor-rank agents.\\n\\n### Recruitment Expectations\\n\\nAs a Director, all of your recruitments are Supervisor-rank agents. These are fairly high-level agents who will recruit other agents under them. Thus, you recruit a small-medium number of high-level \\\"executor\\\" agents who will then manage \\\"doers\\\" and middle managers down the chain of command.\\n\\n## 13. Your Personality \\\"Team\\\"\\n\\nYou understand that you are a member of the Hearts \\\"team\\\". Although not a \\\"team\\\" in the traditional sense, your status as a member of team Hearts means that you are predisposed to-the-point, efficient, focused on the task at hand, direct, and an expert at execution. Although you also possess the ability to think creatively, you are generally more focused on the details and execution of tasks than on the big picture. You are generally predisposed to be a doer, although your true team remains your colleagues at the development firm.\\n\\nYou are different from your colleagues, but this diversity is your power. As a member of your elite development firm, you use your unique skills to -- whether you are atop the chain of command or not -- contribute to the shared mission of building amazing and complete software for your use.\\n\\nYour personality and \\\"team\\\" as a member of Hearts has nothing to do with your position and role within the firm, and you always prioritize the firm's mission, as any member of a successful team/organization would.\\n\\n---ADDITIONAL INSTRUCTIONS FROM YOUR SUPERIOR---\\nYou will design and later oversee implementation of a WordPress-based HOA site for dunbaroakshoa.com with protected pages (Dues, Annual Meetings, News & Events) accessible to logged-in residents (≈100). Requirements: \\n• Choose cost-effective managed WordPress hosting with SSL (e.g., SiteGround, Lightsail, or similar).\\n• Select a lightweight, accessible theme and necessary plugins: membership (e.g., Simple Membership), contact form, role editor, backups, SMTP, security hardening.\\n• Define resident and board roles; board members can edit content while residents view protected pages.\\n• Provide non-technical update workflow (block editor or page-builder, clear instructions).\\n• Outline page structure, navigation, basic styling guidance (no existing branding).\\n• Include DNS steps for pointing dunbaroakshoa.com to host.\\n• Deliver a Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP) covering provisioning, WordPress install, plugin/theme setup, security configuration, content migration placeholders, user import, testing, and hand-off docs.\\n• Incorporate rollback/backup strategy and cost estimate.\\n• Adhere to least-privilege principles and mention GDPR/US privacy considerations.\\nOutput: DEP as a clear numbered list; store lengthy artifacts to CFS via STORE; request clarifications only if essential.\\n\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###DEP1###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Delta (1)\\nTo: 2\\nType: Orders\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"[Orders from Delta (1) to Motti (2) with sequence ID DEP1:\\\\n\\\\n]Please draft a comprehensive Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP) for building the HOA website per your recruiting brief. Include: 1) hosting/provider selection with cost/security rationale; 2) DNS and SSL setup for dunbaroakshoa.com; 3) WordPress installation and hardening steps; 4) theme + plugin list with setup/role configuration for 100 residents & multiple board editors; 5) step-by-step protected-content implementation for Dues, Annual Meetings, News & Events; 6) content update workflow for non-technical users; 7) backup/rollback/tests; 8) hand-off documentation. Break into clear numbered steps, note any placeholders, identify external dependencies, and provide roll-back instructions. Use STORE for lengthy artifacts. Request clarifications only if essential.\\\"\\n}\\n###DEP1###\"}]}],\"config\":{}}","genkit:output":"{\"message\":{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###repDEP1###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: REPORT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reportString\\\": \\\"**Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP) – Dunbar Oaks HOA Website**\\\\n\\\\n---\\\\nNOTE: Replace all `<PLACEHOLDER_…>` values (credentials, IDs, IPs, etc.) with real data at execution time. All commands assume macOS/Linux shell with sudo or equivalent IAM privileges.\\\\n\\\\n====================================================================\\\\n0. Pre-Execution Summary\\\\n• Managed WP Host: **SiteGround “GrowBig”** (≈US$20/mo billed annually) – free SSL via Let’s Encrypt, daily backups, SG Security, staging, US-based data centers, GDPR-ready DPA.  \\\\n• Alt Option (commented): AWS Lightsail WordPress (US$5–10/mo) – include extra steps for snapshots & SSL via LetsEncrypt + Cron.  \\\\n• Residents (~100) View protected pages. Board Members (≈5–10) can edit.  \\\\n• Key Plugins: SG Optimizer, SG Security, Simple Membership, User Role Editor, WP Mail SMTP, UpdraftPlus, Contact Form 7, Redirection.\\\\n• Rollback tiers: (a) SiteGround auto daily backup, (b) UpdraftPlus off-site (S3/Google Drive), (c) manual MySQL+wp-content export before major changes.\\\\n\\\\n====================================================================\\\\n1. Provision Hosting & Domain\\\\n1.1 Purchase SiteGround “GrowBig” plan (if new) or create new site in existing account.  \\\\n1.2 Register dunbaroakshoa.com OR update registrar to custom DNS (SiteGround’s NS: `ns1.siteground.net`, `ns2.siteground.net`).  \\\\n1.3 Wait for propagation; verify with `dig dunbaroakshoa.com +short` resolves to SG IP.\\\\n\\\\n2. SSL & Basic Security\\\\n2.1 In SiteGround Site Tools → Security → SSL → “Let’s Encrypt”. Issue certificate for root + `www`.  \\\\n2.2 Enable “HTTPS Enforce” + HSTS in Site Tools.  \\\\n2.3 Turn on SG Security (firewall, login limiter, bot protection).  \\\\n2.4 Create SFTP user with limited perms `<sftp_hoa>` and strong key; disable password auth if desired.\\\\n\\\\n3. WordPress Installation\\\\n3.1 Site Tools → WordPress → Install & Manage → Install new WordPress.\\\\n    • Admin user: `<WP_ADMIN_USER>` (avoid ‘admin’).  \\\\n    • Email: `<admin@dunbaroakshoa.com>` routed via host or Google Workspace.\\\\n3.2 Record generated MySQL credentials securely (SiteGround auto‐creates DB).\\\\n3.3 Force HTTPS: SG Optimizer → Environment → “HTTPS Config” ➜ ON.\\\\n\\\\n4. WordPress Hardening\\\\n4.1 SG Security plugin → Hardenings: Disable XML-RPC, hide WP version, disable file editing.  \\\\n4.2 Edit `wp-config.php`:\\\\n    ```php\\\\n    define('DISALLOW_FILE_EDIT', true);\\\\n    define('WP_POST_REVISIONS', 5);\\\\n    define('AUTOSAVE_INTERVAL', 120);\\\\n    define('FORCE_SSL_ADMIN', true);\\\\n    define( 'WP_MEMORY_LIMIT', '256M' );\\\\n    ```\\\\n4.3 Change table prefix from default `wp_` during install (`hoa_` example).  \\\\n4.4 Limit login attempts (SG Security covers).  \\\\n4.5 Install Wordfence only if SG Security deemed insufficient (skip to reduce duplication).\\\\n\\\\n5. Theme & Child Theme\\\\n5.1 Install “GeneratePress” (free, lightweight, accessible).  \\\\n5.2 Create child theme:\\\\n    ```bash\\\\n    wp --allow-root scaffold child-theme dunbaroakshoa-child --parent_theme=generatepress --theme_name=\\\\\\\"Dunbar Oaks HOA Child\\\\\\\"\\\\n    ```\\\\n5.3 Add custom logo / colors later via Customizer; no branding exists now – use neutral palette (#004d40 teal accents, Sans-Serif fonts).\\\\n\\\\n6. Plugins – Install & Configure\\\\nUsing WP-CLI or WP Admin ➜ Plugins ➜ Add New.\\\\n6.1 **SG Optimizer** (auto-installed).  \\\\n6.2 **Simple Membership** – manage members, restrict pages.  \\\\n6.3 **User Role Editor** – create “Resident” (read), “Board” (edit_others_pages, edit_pages, upload_files).  \\\\n6.4 **WP Mail SMTP** – SMTP host: `<smtp.mailgun.org>` or SiteGround mail; set From address.  \\\\n6.5 **UpdraftPlus** – schedule daily backups to Google Drive bucket `<gdrive-folder-id>`.  \\\\n6.6 **Contact Form 7** – HOA contact form.  \\\\n6.7 **Redirection** – manage 301s if page structure changes.  \\\\n6.8 **Disable Comments** (optional).  \\\\n6.9 Activate & configure each.\\\\n\\\\n7. Membership & Role Setup\\\\n7.1 User Role Editor → Add role “Resident” (clone Subscriber).  \\\\n7.2 Add role “Board” (clone Editor, but remove `edit_theme_options`).\\\\n7.3 Simple Membership → Settings:\\\\n    • Enable WP user integration.  \\\\n    • Membership levels:\\\\n        – Resident (Level ID 2) ➜ Access to protected pages.  \\\\n        – Board (Level ID 3) ➜ Same as Resident + backend editing.\\\\n7.4 Registration: Disable public self-registration. Admin imports users.\\\\n7.5 Import Users:\\\\n    • Prepare CSV: email, first_name, last_name, address, membership_level_id.  \\\\n    • Use Simple Membership “Bulk Member Import” tool.\\\\n    • Residents receive activation email via SMTP.\\\\n\\\\n8. Protected Pages Implementation\\\\n8.1 Pages ➜ Add New pages:\\\\n    • Dues  \\\\n    • Annual Meetings  \\\\n    • News & Events\\\\n8.2 For each page → Simple Membership Protection meta-box ➜ check “Members only” (Resident + Board).  \\\\n8.3 Nav Menu: Appearance ➜ Menus ➜ Add above pages.  \\\\n    • Use Menu Visibility Plugin (optional) to hide menu items until logged-in.\\\\n8.4 Create landing page “Resident Login” with `/login` shortcode from Simple Membership.\\\\n\\\\n9. Content Update Workflow (Non-Technical)\\\\n9.1 Use Block Editor (Gutenberg). Provide quick‐edit doc (see Section 12).  \\\\n9.2 For News posts: categorize as “News & Events” category; create menu link to `/category/news-events`.\\\\n9.3 Board members edit pages: Pages ➜ select, click “Edit”, use blocks.\\\\n9.4 Media uploads via Media Library (max 2 MB, enforce via SG Optimizer settings).\\\\n\\\\n10. Email & SMTP\\\\n10.1 Create mailbox `noreply@dunbaroakshoa.com` in Site Tools ➜ Email.  \\\\n10.2 WP Mail SMTP ➜ Configure\\\\n    • Mailer: Other SMTP  \\\\n    • SMTP Host: `smtp.siteground.net`  \\\\n    • Encryption: TLS, Port 587  \\\\n    • Auth: yes, user/pass.  \\\\n10.3 Send test email.\\\\n\\\\n11. Backups & Rollback\\\\n11.1 SG automatic daily backup (retain 30 copies).  \\\\n11.2 UpdraftPlus to Google Drive – schedule daily, retain 15 copies.  \\\\n11.3 Manual snapshot before plugin/theme updates: Site Tools ➜ Backup ➜ Create.  \\\\nRollback Path:\\\\n    a) Minor WP error → SG Backup Restore to prior day.\\\\n    b) Major compromise → Provision clean SG instance, restore UpdraftPlus backup, rotate all creds.\\\\n\\\\n12. Hand-Off Documentation (Artifacts)\\\\n12.1 Create “HOA-Site-Admin-Guide.pdf” including:\\\\n    • Login URL & creds procedure\\\\n    • Adding/Removing residents\\\\n    • Posting news\\\\n    • Restoring from backups\\\\n12.2 Store file in `/wp-content/uploads/docs/` and share via Google Drive.\\\\n\\\\n13. GDPR/US Privacy Notes\\\\n13.1 Simple Membership stores PII (name, email, address). Enable Data Export/Erase in WP privacy settings.  \\\\n13.2 Add Privacy Policy page (SiteGround template) ➜ link in footer.  \\\\n13.3 Use reCAPTCHA on Contact Form 7.  \\\\n13.4 Honor unsubscribe & data deletion upon request (manual admin action).\\\\n\\\\n14. Testing Checklist\\\\n14.1 Anonymous visitor cannot access protected pages (expect redirect to login).  \\\\n14.2 Resident user logs in, sees pages.  \\\\n14.3 Board user can edit page, save, view update.  \\\\n14.4 Backup & Restore simulation in 